{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789902528,"nonce":"wU7fKiFhA9kpOIbFQ0yJDSlh/Ss=","title":"Frame records cost ~10% on binary_trees; a frame-pointer walk would be free","message":"Since 61e0fa7755f7559ff9d7c6eb59dc22228989e1ef every compiled body that can raise links a 16-byte record in its frame (load head, store prev, store head; store prev at return; one key store per call site). On the NUC that is ~10% on bench/binary_trees and bench/ash/binary_trees (recursive check with two direct calls), within noise elsewhere.\n\nThe zero-cost design: keep frame pointers in compiled bodies (Cranelift preserve_frame_pointers, LLVM frame-pointer=all), record (fp, key) only at calls into runtime helpers, save the exit pair around each entry from the VM, and map return addresses of direct compiled calls to (func_id, site) through per-body tables: Cranelift srclocs set to the site before each call (MachBufferFinalized::get_srclocs_sorted), LLVM stackmaps or patchpoints read from the __LLVM_StackMaps section (needs a way to find it: inkwell 0.8 has no MCJIT memory-manager hook; check whether MCJIT's symbol lookup resolves __LLVM_StackMaps). Direct calls then cost nothing; the walk happens only at a raise.","files":null}]}